            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"Iron broadly symbolizes strength, hardness, and crushing or unyielding power, often representing severe judgment, absolute rule (such as the messianic 'rod of iron'), or oppressive kingdoms (specifically the fourth empire in Daniel 2).\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"Iron symbolizes the fourth kingdom in Daniel's prophecies, specifically identified as the Roman Empire.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Strength, unyielding power, and crushing force, including its application to oppressive empires.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The fourth kingdom (Rome) in Daniel's prophetic sequence.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUBSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book restricts the symbol of iron exclusively to the fourth kingdom (Rome) of Daniel's vision, whereas the consensus sees it as a broader symbol of strength and power that includes Rome alongside other referents like messianic rule, affliction (the iron furnace), and stubbornness.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"None. The book elevates a specific prominent application (Daniel's fourth kingdom) recognized by the consensus into the primary definition.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The consensus acknowledges Daniel's fourth empire (traditionally Rome) as a major instantiation of iron's broader symbolic meaning of crushing strength. The book narrows the definition by equating iron entirely with this specific prophetic referent.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"Iron is used in many other biblical contexts that cannot refer to Rome, such as the messianic 'rod of iron' (Psalm 2:9) or the 'iron furnace' of Egyptian bondage (Deut 4:20).\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
